From 4a8be3533fa04b31ce21b1f0e177ab2c28ac0a00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:24:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] JavaScriptCore: whole functions, calls, property access and
 doubles in the 68k template JIT (J4)

- Whole functions: the function prologues enter a CodeBlock's code at its
  op_enter when it has some; otherwise op_enter counts calls towards
  compiling it, and the replace slow path (reached from calls and returns)
  compiles it instead of giving up on the CodeBlock. Before, only loops
  reached the JIT, and a function that returned often stopped its own loops
  from being compiled.
- Calls: op_enter, op_ret, op_call and op_construct get their own code.
  op_enter has the local count, scope register and checks known at compile
  time (its template is the slow path); op_ret skips the count towards
  compiling; op_call and op_construct set up the callee's frame and enter
  it through the call link's monomorphic destination when the callee is
  the cached one, and return into the compiled code.
- The 68k interpreter no longer fills value, arithmetic or argument
  profiles: only the optimizing JITs read them, and the 68k has none.
- Property access: get_by_id and put_by_id get their own code for sites
  whose interpreter cache holds an own property (a replace, for puts). The
  code reads the cache as it runs, so the garbage collector clearing it or
  the slow path changing it stays safe; storing a cell takes the slow path
  unless the object needs no write barrier.
- Doubles: add, sub, mul and the compare-and-jumps work on the FPU when an
  operand is not an int32 or an int32 result overflows, out of line, before
  the slow path; a double constant operand gets the FPU code inline.
  fdadd/fdsub/fdmul round once to double, as JavaScript asks; the
  interpreter's double arithmetic now uses the same instructions
  (fdadd/fdsub/fdmul/fddiv/fdsqrt), so both give the same results. 68040
  and up.

useM68KPropertyFastPaths (default true) turns the property code off.
